{"seq":1,"at":"2026-09-17","kind":"imported","target":{"type":"article","id":"a0820"},"reason":"Article of the Quiztopia bank (written for card c069): researched and transcribed in September 2026, converted to the quiz platform on 2026-10-20.","by":"Quiztopia import","ref":"quiz-archive/transcripts/quiztopia/c069.json"},
{"seq":2,"at":"2026-10-20","kind":"edited","target":{"type":"article","id":"a0820"},"reason":"Rewritten by the merge to take in the printed cards' questions.","by":"merge film-media/film","ref":"quiz-archive/runs/tp-ingest/decisions/film-media__film.g2.json","changes":[{"field":"bodyEn","diff":[["=",289],["+"," Germany returned the affection: in 2011 the town of Schwäbisch Gmünd, where Pedersoli had won a 100-metre freestyle race in 1951, renamed its open-air pool in the Schießtal the Bud-Spencer-Bad, after an online vote to name a road tunnel after him had not been adopted, and the actor came to open it himself."],["=",157],["+","Germany produced its own famous reinvention. Klaus Günter Karl Nakszynski, born in 1926 in Zoppot near Danzig, began acting after the Second World War under the name Klaus Kinski and became the wild-eyed star of Edgar Wallace thrillers, spaghetti Westerns and Werner Herzog's „Aguirre, the Wrath of God“ and „Fitzcarraldo“.\n\n"],["=",390],["+","Not every famous name needed changing. Richard Gere, born in Philadelphia in 1949, kept the one he was given – including a middle name that surprises most of his fans: Tiffany. Richard Tiffany Gere became a star with „American Gigolo“ (1980) and „Pretty Woman“ (1990) without ever hiding behind a pseudonym.\n\n"],["=",80],["+","Klaus Günter Karl Nakszynski | Klaus Kinski | adopted after the war |\n| "],["=",115]]},{"field":"bodyDe","diff":[["=",283],["+"," Deutschland erwiderte die Zuneigung: 2011 benannte die Stadt Schwäbisch Gmünd, wo Pedersoli 1951 ein Rennen über 100 Meter Freistil gewonnen hatte, ihr Freibad im Schießtal in Bud-Spencer-Bad um, nachdem ein Online-Votum für einen „Bud-Spencer-Tunnel“ nicht umgesetzt worden war, und der Schauspieler kam zur Einweihung selbst."],["=",169],["+","Auch Deutschland brachte eine berühmte Neuerfindung hervor. Klaus Günter Karl Nakszynski, 1926 in Zoppot bei Danzig geboren, stand nach dem Zweiten Weltkrieg unter dem Namen Klaus Kinski auf der Bühne und wurde zum Star mit dem irren Blick in Edgar-Wallace-Krimis, Italowestern und Werner Herzogs „Aguirre, der Zorn Gottes“ und „Fitzcarraldo“.\n\n"],["=",378],["+","Nicht jeder berühmte Name musste geändert werden. Richard Gere, 1949 in Philadelphia geboren, behielt den, den er bekommen hatte – samt eines zweiten Vornamens, der die meisten seiner Fans überrascht: Tiffany. Richard Tiffany Gere wurde mit „American Gigolo“ (1980) und „Pretty Woman“ (1990) zum Star, ohne sich je hinter einem Pseudonym zu verstecken.\n\n"],["=",70],["+","Klaus Günter Karl Nakszynski | Klaus Kinski | nach dem Krieg angenommen |\n| "],["=",107]]}]},
